<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  An <exercises> DIVISION (not a <subsection>).

  Why: exercises here get their own counter, starting at 1, instead of sharing
  the section-wide block counter with theorems and examples. They are also named
  "Exercise" natively, so no <rename> is needed in <docinfo>.

  The division is titled "Exercises" by default; the <title> below is optional
  and can be deleted.

  Widget page lives at:  assets/iframe/hint-extrema-triangle.html
  (PreTeXt copies "assets" into the build as "external", so @iframe starts at
  "iframe/" — do not add a directory prefix.)

  To hide solutions until a release date, put this in publication.ptx:
      <common>
        <exercise-divisional solution="no"/>
      </common>
  Note: "divisional", not "inline", now that the exercise lives in a division.
-->

<exercises xml:id="exercises-absolute-extrema">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-extrema-triangle">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
        <me>f(x,y) = x^2 - xy + y^2 - 3y</me>
        on the region bounded by the <m>x</m>- and <m>y</m>-axes and the line
        <m>x + y = 4</m>.
      </p>

      <interactive xml:id="hints-extrema-triangle"
                   width="80%"
                   aspect="5:4"
                   iframe="iframe/hint-extrema-triangle.html"/>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The gradient is <m>\nabla f = \langle 2x - y,\; -x + 2y - 3\rangle</m>, which
        vanishes only at <m>(1,2)</m>, a point in the interior of the region. There
        <m>f(1,2) = -3</m>. Since this is the only critical point on all of
        <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> and it is a minimum (by the second derivative test), it is a
        global minimum. We now examine the three boundary segments to look for possible
        maxima.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>x</m>-axis with <m>0 \le x \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(x,0) = x^2</m>, which
        is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        On the <m>y</m>-axis with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have <m>f(0,y) = y^2 - 3y</m>,
        which is maximized at <m>f(0,4) = 4</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, on the line <m>x = 4 - y</m> with <m>0 \le y \le 4</m>, we have
        <m>f(4-y,\,y) = 3y^2 - 15y + 16</m>, which is maximized at <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Altogether, there is an absolute minimum <m>f(1,2) = -3</m> and an absolute maximum
        <m>f(4,0) = 16</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
